# The true potential of `policy`: not "write `allow` right next to the
# `requires` I just declared" -- policy_library.sns declares NO policy at
# all, on purpose. This file imports that shared library and decides, in
# three completely independent scopes, what each one is actually allowed
# to do with it -- without changing a single line of the library's code.
#
# The mechanism that makes this real, not cosmetic: a capability check
# resolves against the *caller's* environment at the call site
# (Interpreter._call_tool/_verify_action/_commit_action all take `env`
# from wherever the call happened), not the tool's own declaration-time
# scope. `policy` lives on `Environment` and is scoped/inherited exactly
# like a variable -- a `session`'s own `policy:` block only ever applies
# inside that session, and never leaks to its siblings or back out.
#
# In a real system, "the library" is written by a platform/security team
# (or is a third-party package), and each session below is a different
# caller with a different trust level -- a support tier, an on-call
# engineer, an autonomous agent -- deciding independently what it may do
# with the exact same shared capabilities.

import "./policy_library.sns" as lib

# -- Scope 1: first-line support. Can email a receipt, but has no
# authority to actually move money.
session first_line_support:
    policy:
        deny payment.refund
        allow email.send

    receipt = lib.send_receipt("customer@example.com", "ORD-104")
    print("[first line] " + receipt)

    # Uncomment to see policy actually stop it -- this crashes the
    # script right here with SensePolicyError, proving the denial is
    # real, not just documentation:
    # lib.refund_customer("ORD-104", 40)

# -- Scope 2: senior support. Exact same imported `refund_customer` --
# nothing about its code changed -- just a broader policy around the call.
session senior_support:
    policy:
        allow payment.refund
        allow email.send

    refund = lib.refund_customer("ORD-104", 40)
    print("[senior] " + refund)

# -- Scope 3: incident response. Needs `data.delete`, which neither
# support scope above has -- and still goes through the full
# prepare -> verify -> commit lifecycle, since it's an `action`, not a `tool`.
session incident_response:
    policy:
        allow data.delete

    cleanup = lib.delete_customer_data("cust-9981")
    cleanup.verify()
    cleanup.commit()

# Outside every session, none of these three policies apply -- capability
# resolution has already walked back out to whatever the top-level
# default is (allow, since nothing here sets one). That's exactly why
# real autonomous/agentic code should run inside a session with its own
# explicit, restrictive policy, rather than relying on the top-level
# default to protect anything.
